@using Yello.Client.Refusals

@inject IStringLocalizer<ClientCopy> Localizer

@*
    THE REFUSAL SURFACE. AC9; UX-DR32, UX-DR35; EXPERIENCE.md:122-147.

    A PAGE, NEVER A TOAST. A toast implies a transient fault and a refusal is a final answer, so this
    is a whole surface with an <h1>, which App.razor's FocusOnNavigate moves focus to.

    ONE PAGE FOR EVERY NOT-FOUND. A Space the Account is not in, a Space that does not exist and an
    address that matches nothing render this component with the same key, the same words, the same
    layout and the same focus. There is no parameter that could distinguish them, because RefusalCopy
    resolves every one of them to the same resource.

    THE CLIENT OWNS EVERY STRING. Nothing here renders a value the server sent except the bound's
    number, which is data. No `danger` colour: a refusal is not an error (DESIGN.md:501).

    THE ROUTE BACK IS HOME, never a remembered last Space - which may itself be the one refused.
    Story 1.7 points home at a Space drawn from the switcher.
*@

<div class="auth-surface">
    <div class="auth-panel">
        <h1 class="type-dialog-title auth-panel__heading">@Heading</h1>
        <p class="auth-panel__aside"><a href="@ClientRoutes.Home">@Localizer[RefusalCopy.RouteBackKey]</a></p>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The problem the refusal arrived with, or <c>null</c> for an address the client cannot route.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public ProblemResponse? Problem { get; set; }

    private string Heading
    {
        get
        {
            var message = RefusalCopy.For(Problem);

            return message.Limit is { } limit
                ? Localizer[message.Key, limit]
                : Localizer[message.Key];
        }
    }
}
